<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwanza River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cuanza River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kwanza is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, about 960 kilometres long, that rises in the central highlands and flows north and west to the Atlantic south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>. From the 16th century it was the main route of Portuguese expansion inland, with forts such as Massangano and Cambambe along its banks. Its name was given to Angola's currency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwanza-river.jpg" alt="Kwanza River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jlrsousa · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuanza_river_near_Dondo,_Angola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
